LATENT DISPATCH
Thu Sep 17 · 4 items · ~3 min

ANATOMY OF A $40K INFERENCE MONTH
A Series A company shared its August bill breakdown with us (anonymized). 55% was a one-time backfill for two new customers; 30% was steady-state extraction; 15% was retries and a runaway summarization job. Their steady-state cost is fine. Their forecast was not, because it did not model backfills as a separate line. Same lesson as last week, with numbers. https://latentdispatch.ai/p/40k-month

QUILLON RAISES $200M
Use of funds: compute and a document-model team. Quillon-M has been the quiet success of the summer for anyone processing paper.

THE FRONTIER GAP IS NARROWING ON THE TASKS THAT PAY THE BILLS
Our quarterly look at the gap between the best proprietary model and the best open model, by task family. Extraction: 2 points. Classification: 1 point. Tool use: 3 points. Hard reasoning: 14 points. If your workload is the first three, the open model is the rational default now. https://latentdispatch.ai/p/frontier-gap-q3

SHORT TAKES
· Argent adds a 24-hour batch tier at 50% off. Everyone has one now.
· Larkspur 1.2: native cost tracking per state.
· Ostrava's document model gets a handwriting update. Still not great; better.

